# CAP-4W hashes and applies this canonical patch byte-for-byte on Windows.
tools/cap4w/webview2-custom-scheme.patch -text whitespace=-trailing-space

# CAP-7L: every Linux gate is a bash script executed from the repository
# working tree - under WSL on the dev host, natively on the ubuntu-24.04
# runner. A CRLF checkout (Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true)
# would break the shebang line and every heredoc, so pin these to LF
# regardless of platform or local git configuration.
*.sh text eol=lf

# CAP-7M1: the macOS runtime fixture is ONE corpus served by TWO stores, and
# the ZIP is built from the same working-tree bytes the folder store reads.
# A line-ending rewrite on checkout would change the served bytes (and the
# archive's CRCs) depending on whose machine checked it out.
#
# The DEFAULT for the whole subtree is binary, and the text members opt back
# in by extension. That direction is deliberate: naming each file individually
# means the next fixture added silently inherits nothing, and the one asset
# whose entire purpose is to prove that every byte value survives the seam is
# exactly the kind of file someone adds without editing this list.
test/cap7m/fixture/** -text
test/cap7m/fixture/**/*.html text eol=lf
test/cap7m/fixture/**/*.css text eol=lf
test/cap7m/fixture/**/*.js text eol=lf
test/cap7m/fixture/**/*.json text eol=lf

# CAP-7F: the frontend sources that ship VERBATIM inside app.pwb. The react
# build cpSync's src/index.html into dist/ (the pas2js build copies its
# sibling the same way) and pwebbundle packages those exact bytes, so a CRLF
# checkout - Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, on the hosted
# windows runner as on the dev host - made the Windows logical bundle
# inventory diverge from the Linux/macOS one by exactly the carriage returns
# (MEASURED: index.html 188 bytes CRLF vs 177 LF; app.js and manifest.json
# already byte-identical cross-OS). The committed corpus is ONE set of bytes
# and every platform must check out, build and ship that same set; the
# CAP-7F cross-OS inventory equality gate is what caught the divergence and
# is what now keeps it caught. The blob store is already LF (i/lf), so this
# changes only the checkout, never the committed content.
#
# Pinned as a CLASS, by text extension, under both frontend src trees: the
# next .css or .json added beside index.html would otherwise inherit nothing
# and reopen the exact divergence this block closes (the cap7m fixture block
# above states the same rationale). Deliberately extension-scoped - a
# potential binary (an image, a font) must never be declared text.
examples/04-react/frontend/src/**/*.html text eol=lf
examples/04-react/frontend/src/**/*.css text eol=lf
examples/04-react/frontend/src/**/*.js text eol=lf
examples/04-react/frontend/src/**/*.json text eol=lf
examples/04-react/frontend/src/**/*.svg text eol=lf
examples/05-pas2js/frontend/src/**/*.html text eol=lf
examples/05-pas2js/frontend/src/**/*.css text eol=lf
examples/05-pas2js/frontend/src/**/*.js text eol=lf
examples/05-pas2js/frontend/src/**/*.json text eol=lf
examples/05-pas2js/frontend/src/**/*.svg text eol=lf
# CAP-9C2: the plugin-enabled acceptance frontend, for the identical
# reason - its build cpSync's src/index.html into dist/ verbatim, so a
# CRLF checkout would make the Windows app.pwb logical inventory diverge
# from the Linux/macOS one by exactly the carriage returns.
examples/07-quickjs/frontend/src/**/*.html text eol=lf
examples/07-quickjs/frontend/src/**/*.css text eol=lf
examples/07-quickjs/frontend/src/**/*.js text eol=lf
examples/07-quickjs/frontend/src/**/*.json text eol=lf
examples/07-quickjs/frontend/src/**/*.svg text eol=lf

# CAP-9C1: the QuickJS plugin sources that ship VERBATIM inside
# plugins.zip, and the trusted build-time list that names them. The
# packager hashes these exact bytes into the semantic inventory, the
# per-plugin module-graph digest and the generated native registry, all
# three of which the four-target evidence compares for equality. A CRLF
# checkout - Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, on the
# hosted windows runner as on the dev host - would make the Windows
# inventory diverge from the Linux/macOS one by exactly the carriage
# returns, which is the same divergence the CAP-7F block above closes
# for the frontend corpus (MEASURED there, and the same mechanism here).
#
# Pinned as a CLASS by text extension, like the two blocks above: the
# next .js or .json added under a plugin root would otherwise inherit
# nothing and reopen the divergence. Deliberately extension-scoped - a
# potential binary must never be declared text.
examples/07-quickjs/plugins/**/*.js text eol=lf
examples/07-quickjs/plugins/**/*.json text eol=lf
examples/07-quickjs/plugins.trusted text eol=lf

# CAP-10B0: the trusted template source tree and the list that declares it.
# These bytes ARE the product: the pack builder hashes every one of them into
# the semantic inventory, the pack SHA-256 and the generated native registry,
# and the four-target evidence compares all three for equality. Unlike every
# earlier block this one also has to survive a SECOND rule - the engine
# refuses a CR byte anywhere in a text template outright - so a CRLF checkout
# does not merely diverge here, it fails the build on Windows and passes
# everywhere else, which is the least useful shape a failure can have.
#
# The DEFAULT for the subtree is binary and the text kinds opt back in by
# extension, matching the test/cap7m fixture block above and for the same
# reason: assets/bytes.bin exists precisely to prove that every byte value
# survives the seam, and it is exactly the kind of file someone adds without
# editing this list. `templates.list` and the two extension-less dot-file
# sources are named individually because they have no extension to match.
tools/templates/** -text
tools/templates/**/*.md text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.txt text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.json text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.js text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.mjs text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.ts text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.tsx text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.css text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.html text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.svg text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.lpr text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.lpi text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.pas text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.inc text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/*.sh text eol=lf
tools/templates/templates.list text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/gitignore text eol=lf
tools/templates/**/gitattributes text eol=lf
